course: dictionary
lesson: 14494
title: Peal: a loud ringing sound
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Peal is a word with a big, joyful sound. Look at the words around it to find its meaning. «The church bells began to peal for the wedding.» The bells are ringing, and the wedding tells you it is a happy time, so peal means to ring loudly.
explain: Peal can also be a noun, meaning a loud sound. «A peal of laughter filled the kitchen.» Laughter is a sound, and filled the kitchen tells you it was loud. So a peal of laughter means a loud burst of laughter.
explain: Test your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Try «The church bells began to ring loudly for the wedding.» It still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: Peal means a loud ringing sound of bells, or a loud sound like thunder or laughter.
fact: As a verb, peal means to ring loudly: «The bells pealed at midnight.»
fact: Bells peal in church for weddings, on Sundays and at Christmas.
fact: In The Wind in the Willows, the field mice come carol-singing to Mole's door at Christmas.
fact: «A peal of thunder» means a loud rumble of thunder, and «a peal of laughter» means a loud, joyful burst of laughter.
exercise: «The church bells began to peal for the wedding.» What does peal mean here? | To fall gently like snow | To hide under the bed | To paint the walls | *To ring loudly
exercise: «A peal of laughter filled the kitchen.» What is a peal of laughter? | A quiet, sad sigh | A tiny whispered joke | A long walk in the rain | *A loud, joyful burst of laughter
exercise: «The bells will peal on Christmas morning.» Find and copy the word that tells you the bells will ring out. | Christmas | morning | *peal | bells
test: «The bells pealed at midnight.» Is pealed a noun or a verb? | adjective | name | *verb | noun
test: «A peal of thunder woke the children.» What does peal mean here? | *A loud, rumbling sound | A quiet whisper | A soft, gentle breeze | A small bright light
exam: «The bells pealed for the wedding, and everyone cheered.» Why did the bells peal? | *Because it was a happy celebration | Because the church was on fire | Because it was time for school | Because the bells were broken
